from __future__ import annotations

NAME = "The Watercourse Way"
AMBIENCE = "Mountain Stream"

# A soothing sleep-lecture about gentle Taoist wisdom in the spirit of Lao Tzu -- wu wei, water as the soft teacher, finding the low quiet place.
SECTIONS = [['You are lying down now, and there is nothing left for you to do.', 'Let the bed take all of your weight, and let yourself be held.', 'Your head is heavy, and the pillow is soft beneath it.', 'There is nothing to reach for and nowhere you need to be.', 'Somewhere not far away, you can imagine the soft sound of water moving.', 'It trickles gently over smooth stones, easy and unhurried.', 'You do not have to listen closely, for the sound simply comes to you.', 'With each breath out, you sink a little deeper into the warmth.', 'Your shoulders soften, and they let go of the day.', 'Your hands rest open and still, asking for nothing.', 'Let your breath grow slow and quiet, like that gentle stream.', 'You are safe here, and you are allowed to rest.'], ['There is an old and gentle way of looking at the world, soft as flowing water.', 'An old Chinese teacher wrote of it long ago, and called it simply the way.', 'He noticed that the world goes on by itself, turning quietly without being pushed.', 'The sun rises without anyone lifting it, and the river runs without being told.', 'You do not have to make your heart beat, for it beats on its own.', 'You do not have to remember to breathe, for the breath breathes itself.', 'So much is already happening for you, gently, while you simply rest.', 'The Taoist sages taught that the truest action can feel like no effort at all.', 'They called it letting things unfold the way they already long to unfold.', 'You need not force the night, for sleep comes the way the tide comes in.', 'Tonight there is nothing to strive for, and nothing to hurry along.', 'Let it all happen by itself, the way it always quietly has.'], ['The old teacher loved water above all things, and called it the great gentle teacher.', 'Water never argues, and it never strains, and yet nothing can resist it.', 'It is the softest thing there is, soft enough to slip through your fingers.', 'And still, in its own slow time, it wears away the hardest stone.', 'It does not hurry the stone, and it does not push against it.', 'It simply keeps flowing, patient and yielding, and the stone gives way.', 'There is a quiet strength in being soft, the sages taught.', 'You do not have to be hard tonight to be strong.', 'You can be soft like water, yielding to the bed and the dark.', 'Let yourself give way the way the stream gives way around a rock.', 'Bending is not breaking, and softness is not weakness.', 'Soften now, and let the gentle current carry you on.'], ['Water has another lesson, the old way teaches, in the way it always travels.', 'It never seeks the high and shining places, and it never climbs.', 'It always finds the low and quiet places, and it settles there to rest.', 'It flows downward, into the valley, into the still and shaded pool.', 'And in that low place it grows calm, and deep, and clear.', 'The sages taught that there is great peace in being low and humble.', 'To be like the valley is to be open, and empty, and at ease.', 'You do not have to rise to anything tonight, or hold yourself up.', 'You can sink, gently, into the lowest and softest place there is.', 'Let yourself settle the way water settles into the quiet hollow.', 'There is nothing beneath you to fear, only rest, and more rest.', 'Find your low quiet place now, and let yourself pool there.'], ['The old teacher spoke too of simplicity, plain and uncarved and whole.', 'He loved the wood before the carver shapes it, still and full of quiet.', 'There is a deep ease in being simple, and asking for nothing more.', 'You do not need to be clever now, or finished, or perfectly made.', 'You can be plain and open, like an empty bowl that holds the stillness.', 'What makes a bowl useful, the sages noticed, is the empty space within.', 'What makes a room restful is the open space where nothing stands.', 'So let your mind grow empty and open, with room for the quiet.', 'You need not fill the dark with thoughts, or carve the night into plans.', 'Let the day go uncarved, and let yourself be simple and at rest.', 'There is nothing missing in this plainness, only peace.', 'Rest in the empty space, soft and unhurried and whole.'], ['When water grows muddy and troubled, the old way asks, what should you do?', 'You do not stir it, and you do not reach in to clear it by hand.', 'You simply leave it alone, and let it be still.', 'And slowly, all on its own, the mud drifts down and settles.', 'The water grows clear again, not because you cleared it, but because you let it rest.', 'Your mind is like this water, the sages taught, clear beneath the stirring.', 'You do not have to fix your thoughts tonight, or chase them clear.', 'Let them settle on their own, the way the silt sinks slowly down.', 'Stop grasping, stop reaching, and let your hands fall open.', 'Floating is easier than holding on, and the water will carry you.', 'Let go of trying to steer, and simply drift on the gentle current.', 'Be still now, and let the quiet come back all by itself.'], ['So let yourself become soft and yielding, soft all the way through.', 'Soft as water, asking for nothing, resisting nothing at all.', 'Let your breath flow slow and low, in its own gentle time.', 'You are sinking now into the lowest and quietest of places.', 'Down into the valley, down into the still and shaded pool.', 'The world is turning on without you, and it needs no help tonight.', 'The seasons turn, the tides come in, your heart beats soft and slow.', 'There is nothing for you to push, and nothing for you to hold.', 'Let the muddy water of the day settle clear, all by itself.', 'You are floating now, carried gently on a current that knows the way.', 'Drift lower, and softer, and quieter, into the warm and welcoming dark.', 'Let the way carry you home, and sleep, and rest, and sleep.']]
